window.docContents[100].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs-results":{"contents":"* colorable_of_forall_finite_subgraph_colorable: the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem, a\ngraph is n-colourable if all its finite subgraphs are.* colorable_of_degree_le: a locally finite graph of maximum degree at most Δ is\n(Δ + 1)-colourable.* Subgraph.IsMatching.le_matchingNum / matchingNum_le /\nexists_isMatching_matchingNum: the basic API for the matching number ν G (lower\nbounds from matchings, upper bounds from uniform bounds, attainment).* Subgraph.IsMatching.ncard_inter_le: in a graph that is bipartite with parts p₁\nand p₂, a finite matching has at least as many edges as it covers vertices of\np₁.* matchingNum_le_card_div_two / Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.matchingNum_eq: a\nfinite graph has ν G ≤ |V| / 2, with equality if it has a perfect matching.* matchingNum_le_vertexCoverNum: weak duality ν G ≤ τ G.* matchingNum_hypercube_succ: ν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n.* two_mul_matchingNum_le_matchingNum_mycielskian /\nSubgraph.IsPerfectMatching.matchingNum_mycielskian_eq: 2 * ν G ≤ ν mμ[G], with\nequality if G has a perfect matching.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* Walk.exists_odd_isCycle_toSubgraph_le / Walk.exists_odd_isCycle_length_le: a\nclosed walk of odd length k contains an odd cycle that is a subgraph of the walk,\nand so has length at most k.* Walk.IsTrail.length_le_of_toSubgraph_le: a trail that is a subgraph of a walk is\nno longer than the walk.* Subgraph.mem_verts_iff_of_isCompl: if subgraphs H and K are complementary\n(IsCompl H K) then no edge of G has exactly one endpoint in H.* colorable_of_forall_finite_induce_colorable: a graph is n-colourable if all its\nfinite induced subgraphs G.induce s are.* matchingNum_mono: the matching number is monotone in the subgraph order.* matchingNum_pathNat₁: the infinite path has matching number ⊤.* indepNum_add_matchingNum_le: in a finite graph ⍺ G + ν G ≤ |V|.* matchingNum_exists: the matching number is attained in every graph, finite or\nnot; when ν G = ⊤ a greedy chain of matchings produces an infinite one.* colorableOn_iff_colorable_induce: for n > 0, G.ColorableOn n s iff\nG.induce s is n-colourable.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Results","header":"Chapter 9: Subgraphs (Results)","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs-results"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-vertices":{"contents":"The vertex set of a SimpleGraph V is V (although strictly speaking V is a type,\nnot a set). These can be infinite (ℕ), or empty (Fin 0). Nonemptiness and\nfiniteness are extra hypotheses that need to be stated: for example as [Nonempty V]\nand [Fintype V]. See Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees for more details.\n\nMathlib defines a number of useful subsets of V (terms s : Set V), in\nparticular the support of the graph and the neighborSet of a vertex. (We can\nrefer to the entire vertex set of a SimpleGraph V as a set by\nSet.univ : Set V.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.3. Vertices","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-vertices"}});